\section{The shared I/O log}
\label{sec:iolog-cap}

The central piece is [[IOLog]]---a thread-safe buffer that collects
lines from all three streams in the order they arrive.
We use a single [[threading.Lock]] so that a write from stdout and a
read from stdin cannot interleave within a single line.

We cap the buffer at a configurable maximum size (default 1~MB) to
prevent a long-running program from consuming unbounded memory.
Once the cap is reached, we stop recording but continue passing data
through to the original streams---the student's program still works
normally.

The cap bounds memory, and nothing else.
It is worth saying so explicitly, because for a while it also had to
absorb a limit it was never designed for: the captured log ends up in a
commit message, and while messages travelled in [[argv]] anything past
128\,KiB destroyed the whole trailer
(\cref{sec:commit-message-stdin}).
Shrinking the cap below that would have hidden the symptom by throwing
away student output instead---a poor trade for a log whose purpose is to
keep that output.
Now that message text reaches Git on standard input, the only question
the cap has to answer is how much of a runaway program's output we are
willing to hold in memory, and one megabyte remains a reasonable answer.

<<constants>>=
MAX_IOLOG_BYTES = 1_000_000
@

\subsection{ANSI escape code stripping}
\label{ansi-stripping}

Starting with Python~3.13, the interpreter emits coloured tracebacks
using ANSI escape codes (CSI sequences such as \verb|\x1b[31m| for red
text).
These sequences are meaningful to a terminal emulator but are
unreadable noise when stored as plain text in a Git commit message.
We therefore strip escape sequences from captured text before
buffering it, while still passing the raw (coloured) text through to
the student's terminal unchanged.

Colour codes are only the easiest family to recognise, and matching
just them is not enough.
As soon as a student uses [[curses]], [[rich]], [[tqdm]], or any
library that repaints the terminal, the captured text also contains
window titles, cursor visibility toggles, alternate-screen switches,
and character-set designations.
Those all sit outside the \enquote{digits and a letter} shape, so a
pattern written for colours leaves them behind and the commit message
still reads as garbage.
Rather than enumerating the sequences we happen to have seen, we
implement the grammar that ECMA-48 (equivalently, ISO/IEC~6429)
actually defines, because that grammar tells us where each sequence
\emph{ends}---which is the only information stripping needs.

The grammar splits escape sequences into four families, and we write
one alternative per family:

\begin{description}
\item[Control sequences (CSI)] \verb|\x1b[| followed by parameter
  bytes, then intermediate bytes, then one final byte that terminates
  the sequence.
\item[Command strings] \verb|\x1b]| (OSC) and \verb|\x1bP|,
  \verb|\x1bX|, \verb|\x1b^|, \verb|\x1b_| (DCS, SOS, PM, APC), which
  carry a free-form payload and are terminated by an explicit string
  terminator instead of by a final byte.
\item[Escape sequences with intermediates] \verb|\x1b| followed by
  intermediate bytes and a final byte, e.g.\ the character-set
  designation \verb|\x1b(B|.
\item[Two-character escapes] \verb|\x1b| followed directly by a final
  byte, e.g.\ \verb|\x1bM| (reverse index) or \verb|\x1b7| (save
  cursor).
\end{description}

The families overlap---\verb|\x1b[| and \verb|\x1b]| are also valid
two-character escapes---so the order of the alternatives is
load-bearing: Python's [[re]] prefers the leftmost alternative that
matches, so the string and control families must be written before the
generic escape catch-all.
We precompile the joined pattern into a module-level constant because
[[strip_ansi]] is called on every single write.
Transparency puts a budget on this: the alternation costs more than
the single colour pattern did---measured at roughly 8~ms per megabyte
of output against 0.5~ms before---which stays far below what a student
could notice at the output volumes the 1~MB buffer cap allows.

<<helper functions>>=
_ANSI_ESCAPE_RE = re.compile("|".join([
    <<ANSI escape sequence patterns>>
]))

def strip_ansi(text):
    """Remove ANSI escape sequences from ``text``."""
    return _ANSI_ESCAPE_RE.sub('', text)
@

An operating system command carries a payload---a window title, a
hyperlink, a clipboard blob---and is closed either by a [[BEL]]
(\verb|\x07|, what \texttt{xterm} historically accepted) or by the
string terminator \verb|\x1b\\|.
Excluding both introducer bytes from the payload class is what keeps
the match linear: the payload class and the terminators are disjoint,
so the engine never has to try alternative splits of the same input.
An unterminated OSC simply fails to match here and falls through to
the two-character escape alternative, which removes the introducer and
leaves the payload as readable text---strictly better than dropping
the remainder of the log.

<<ANSI escape sequence patterns>>=
r"\x1b\][^\x07\x1b]*(?:\x07|\x1b\\)",
@

The other string families (DCS for terminal queries, SOS, PM, and APC
for application data such as \texttt{kitty} graphics) accept only the
string terminator, so their payload class need exclude only
\verb|\x1b|.

<<ANSI escape sequence patterns>>=
r"\x1b[PX^_][^\x1b]*\x1b\\",
@

The control sequence introducer takes parameter bytes
(\verb|0x30|--\verb|0x3f|, which is where the private-use marker
\verb|?| of \verb|\x1b[?25l| lives), then intermediate bytes
(\verb|0x20|--\verb|0x2f|, used by e.g.\ the soft reset
\verb|\x1b[!p|), then a final byte (\verb|0x40|--\verb|0x7e|).
Writing the ranges rather than \verb|[0-9;]| and \verb|[A-Za-z]| is
precisely what fixes the private and intermediate forms.

<<ANSI escape sequence patterns>>=
r"\x1b\[[0-?]*[ -/]*[@-~]",
@

The last escape alternative covers both remaining families at once,
because they differ only in whether intermediate bytes are present:
zero or more intermediates (\verb|0x20|--\verb|0x2f|) followed by one
final byte (\verb|0x30|--\verb|0x7e|).
The two ranges are disjoint, so this alternative cannot backtrack
either.

<<ANSI escape sequence patterns>>=
r"\x1b[ -/]*[0-~]",
@

Terminals may also send the C1 controls as single bytes instead of as
two-byte escapes.
We match the 8-bit control sequence introducer \verb|\x9b|, and
deliberately \emph{not} the 8-bit string introducers.
The reason is that [[strip_ansi]] works on [[str]], where \verb|\x9b|
is the code point U+009B and nothing else; had we matched the byte
\verb|0x9b| in a [[bytes]] object it would also occur inside the UTF-8
encoding of ordinary characters (U+061B encodes as \verb|0xd8 0x9b|),
and stripping it would corrupt the text.
Working on decoded text removes that hazard, but not the risk that a
\verb|\x9b| in a [[str]] is the debris of a mis-decode rather than a
real control.
A C1 control sequence is self-delimiting and short, so a false match
costs a few characters; a C1 \emph{string} introducer would run to its
terminator and could swallow the rest of the log.
We therefore take the bounded case only.

<<ANSI escape sequence patterns>>=
r"\x9b[0-?]*[ -/]*[@-~]",
@

We need to verify two directions: that escape codes are actually
stripped (otherwise Python~3.13 tracebacks remain unreadable), and
that plain text passes through unmodified (otherwise we would corrupt
output that happens to contain bracket characters):

<<test functions>>=
def test_strip_ansi_removes_color_codes():
    colored = "\x1b[31mError\x1b[0m: something failed"
    assert strip_ansi(colored) == "Error: something failed"

def test_strip_ansi_preserves_plain_text():
    plain = "hello world\n"
    assert strip_ansi(plain) == plain

def test_strip_ansi_handles_multiple_codes():
    text = "\x1b[1m\x1b[31mBold Red\x1b[0m Normal"
    assert strip_ansi(text) == "Bold Red Normal"

def test_strip_ansi_empty_string():
    assert strip_ansi("") == ""
@

Each of the remaining families needs its own case, because each one
was silently left in the log by the colours-only pattern.
We use [[pytest.mark.parametrize]] so that a family which regresses
names itself in the failure report instead of hiding behind an
assertion further up a shared test body.
The samples are the ones a real full-screen program emits: the
alternate screen and cursor toggles from [[curses]], the window title
that a shell or a progress bar sets, a terminal capability query, a
[[kitty]] graphics payload, and the character-set and cursor-save
escapes that [[less]] and [[vim]] leave behind on exit.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("family,text", [
    ("private CSI", "\x1b[?1049hbody\x1b[?1049l"),
    ("cursor hiding", "\x1b[?25lbody\x1b[?25h"),
    ("CSI intermediates", "\x1b[!pbody"),
    ("OSC with BEL", "\x1b]0;window title\x07body"),
    ("OSC with ST", "\x1b]8;;http://x/\x1b\\body"),
    ("DCS", "\x1bP+q544e\x1b\\body"),
    ("APC", "\x1b_Ga=T,f=100;payload\x1b\\body"),
    ("PM", "\x1b^private\x1b\\body"),
    ("SOS", "\x1bXstring\x1b\\body"),
    ("charset designation", "\x1b(B\x1b)0body\x1b%G"),
    ("two-character escape", "\x1bMbody\x1b7\x1b8"),
    ("keypad mode", "\x1b=body\x1b>"),
    ("full reset", "\x1bcbody"),
    ("8-bit CSI", "\x9b31mbody\x9b0m"),
])
def test_strip_ansi_removes_escape_family(family, text):
    assert strip_ansi(text) == "body"
@

The opposite direction matters just as much.
Extending the grammar widens what we delete, so we pin down that text
which merely \emph{looks} like an escape sequence---brackets, a regular
expression, a shell prompt---survives untouched.
The last sample is written with numeric escapes so that the literate
source stays ASCII; it is the Arabic semicolon U+061B, chosen because
its UTF-8 encoding contains the byte \verb|0x9b| that the 8-bit
alternative matches.
As a decoded [[str]] it is a single code point that the pattern cannot
see, which is exactly the property that makes the 8-bit alternative
safe.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("text", [
    "matches [0-9;]+ in a[1] and b[?25l\n",
    "$ ls ]0;title (B M P _ ^ X\n",
    "a[[b]] and c]]d and e[?]f\n",
    "caf\xe9 \u061b \u2014 done\n",
])
def test_strip_ansi_leaves_escape_free_text_alone(text):
    assert strip_ansi(text) == text
@

A stray escape character with nothing that completes a sequence has no
valid interpretation, and guessing one would delete real output.
Such input is left alone:

<<test functions>>=
def test_strip_ansi_keeps_incomplete_escape():
    assert strip_ansi("tail\x1b") == "tail\x1b"
@

An unterminated command string is the interesting boundary case: the
string alternatives cannot match, so only the introducer is removed
and the payload stays readable rather than the rest of the capture
disappearing with it.

<<test functions>>=
def test_strip_ansi_unterminated_osc_keeps_payload():
    assert strip_ansi("\x1b]0;title") == "0;title"
@

Stripping is only useful if it happens on the path the captured text
actually takes, so one test goes through [[IOLog]] rather than calling
[[strip_ansi]] directly:

<<test functions>>=
def test_iolog_strips_ansi_from_logged_text():
    log = IOLog()
    log.log("stderr", "\x1b[31mTraceback\x1b[0m\n")
